  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the Humanitarian Response to Crisis-Affected Populations in Czechia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 24 February 2022, the Russian Federation launched a broad military offensive against Ukraine, causing the largest population displacement crisis since World War II. Close to six million persons were forced to leave Ukraine, and another 8 million were displaced internally. With over 460,000 Ukrainian citizens applying for temporary protection in Czechia, the country now hosts the highest number of people fleeing the conflict in Ukraine per capita. 
 
To ensure the dignity, safety and protection of vulnerable conflict-affected populations, including refugees, third country nationals, and host communities, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is implementing a multi-sector humanitarian response, supporting the efforts of central and local authorities, civil society organisations, and humanitarian partners and offering a comprehensive package of quality services to the affected populations. Specifically, this will be achieved through the provision of housing assistance, training of community workers on crisis interventions and facilitating social services, and increasing access of refugees to social services.</narrative>
  </description>
